{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. Patel. I’ve been reviewing why you came in today. You had nausea, vomiting, and abdominal discomfort—does that sound right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s correct. It started yesterday with some stomach pain, so I doubled my prednisone, and it got better for a while. But then this morning, I started vomiting—four times—and I just didn’t feel right."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned feeling weak in your legs too?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, both legs. Not numb, just weak. It’s been going on for about two days. I thought maybe I overdid it in the garden."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We also noticed you have some pain in your right calf and a bruise. Can you tell me more about that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, yes. I’ve had some old Achilles tendon issues. I was gardening—kneeling, squatting—and I think I pulled it. And my grandson hit me with his toy jeep—hence the bruise!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. We’re being cautious because with your Addison’s, any stress—even a small injury—can sometimes trigger a flare. But this sounds more like overuse. We’ll check it with an ultrasound just to be safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2164-08-28 13:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s fine. I just want to make sure it’s not something serious."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs came back. Your sodium is low—131—and your potassium is high—5.6. That, along with your symptoms, suggests an Addisonian flare."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Like last time? But I doubled my prednisone already. Why did it still happen?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Sometimes the body needs more than the usual increase. You might have been fighting off a small infection—your daughter had pneumonia recently, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, she was coughing for weeks. I did spend time with her. Could that have triggered it even if I don’t feel sick?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. Your immune system may have been responding even without obvious symptoms. That’s enough stress to tip the balance with Addison’s."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s scary. So what happens now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re giving you IV hydrocortisone—stronger and faster-acting than prednisone—and IV fluids to correct your electrolytes. You already got a dose in the ER and said you felt better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes! I actually feel hungry now. That’s a good sign, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Very good. We’ll start you on a regular diet and keep monitoring your labs. Your kidney function is a bit off too—creatinine is 1.4—but that’s likely from not eating or drinking enough the past couple days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not permanent damage?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2164-08-28 14:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, this looks like prerenal—meaning it’s due to dehydration, not kidney disease. With fluids, it should bounce back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2164-08-28 16:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your chest X-ray came back clear—no pneumonia or infection. That’s reassuring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2164-08-28 16:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ve had a dry cough, but nothing serious."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2164-08-28 16:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep an eye on it, but it doesn’t look like an infection is driving this flare."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning! Your repeat labs look much better. Sodium is up to 139, potassium down to 5.2, and creatinine is back to 1.0. You’re stabilizing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great! And my leg pain is better too. I walked to the bathroom without trouble."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The ultrasound showed no blood clot—good news. We think the pain is from overuse and old tendon issues. We’ll send you home with some pain meds just in case."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. What about my blood pressure? I noticed you held my metoprolol."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, your BP was low when you arrived—110/60—so we paused it. But now that you’re hydrated and your BP is in the 120s, it’s safe to restart. You can take it again today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And my other meds—levothyroxine, Celexa?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You stayed on all your regular meds except metoprolol. We did increase your Celexa to 20mg daily—your doctor thought it might help with anxiety around these flares."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2164-08-29 08:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s fine. I have been more anxious lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you today. You’ll go home on a short course of hydrocortisone pills—50mg every 8 hours for two doses, then taper down over the next two days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Then back to my usual prednisone and Florinef?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. The taper helps your body adjust back to your baseline dose safely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What if I feel off again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Watch for vomiting, severe weakness, dizziness, or confusion. If those happen, go to the ER and take extra steroids if you’re able. Always carry your emergency injection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do. I keep it in my purse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Also, call your PCP soon—within the next week—to review labs and your meds, especially metoprolol."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do that. And thank you—everyone’s been so kind."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re very welcome. You responded well to treatment. Just take it easy at home, stay hydrated, and listen to your body."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2164-08-29 09:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. No more gardening for a few days!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One more thing—we added clonazepam as a PRN for anxiety or trouble sleeping. Take half a tablet if needed, but not more than once every 12 hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll keep it on hand."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Nurse will go over your discharge instructions and meds. You should be ready to leave by noon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Perfect. I’m ready to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. And don’t hesitate to reach out if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I won’t. Thank you again, Dr. Patel."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Have a safe trip home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll follow up with your PCP to make sure everything’s on track."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2164-08-29 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s reassuring."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2164-08-28 13:36:00",
    "admission_end": "2164-08-29 17:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient, a woman with a history of Addison’s disease, hypothyroidism, and hypertension, was admitted for abdominal discomfort, nausea, vomiting, and bilateral lower extremity weakness, consistent with an adrenal crisis. She had doubled her prednisone prior to admission with partial relief. Labs showed hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and acute kidney injury likely due to prerenal causes from poor oral intake. She received IV hydrocortisone and fluids in the emergency department and improved rapidly. Her electrolytes normalized, symptoms resolved, and she tolerated a regular diet. A right lower extremity ultrasound ruled out DVT; leg pain was attributed to overuse and old tendon injury. She was discharged on a 2-day hydrocortisone taper, resumed her baseline prednisone and Florinef, restarted metoprolol, and had Celexa increased to 20mg daily. Follow-up was advised with her primary care provider.",
    "problems": [
      "Glucocorticoid deficiency",
      "Acute kidney failure, unspecified",
      "Hyposmolality and/or hyponatremia",
      "Hyperpotassemia",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Migraine, unspecified, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus",
      "Anemia, unspecified",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified"
    ]
  }
}
